+### Compound Durations
+
+`parseCompound` sums several `<number><unit>` pieces. Pieces can be separated by spaces, commas, or nothing at all.
+
+```ts
+import { parseCompound } from 'ms';
+
+parseCompound('1h 30m'); // 5400000
+parseCompound('1h, 30m'); // 5400000
+parseCompound('2 days 4 hours'); // 187200000
+parseCompound('1h30m15s'); // 5415000
+```
+
+Unlike `parse`, which returns `NaN` for invalid input, `parseCompound` throws a `TypeError` (including the offending input in the message). Every piece must have a unit, so `parseCompound('1h 30')` throws.
+
+### Clock Strings
+
+`parseClock` parses timer-style strings: `m:ss` and `h:mm:ss`.
+
+```ts
+import { parseClock } from 'ms';
+
+parseClock('1:30'); // 90000
+parseClock('01:02:03'); // 3723000
+parseClock('1:75'); // undefined
+```
+
+Seconds (and minutes in `h:mm:ss`) must be two digits below 60. Invalid input returns `undefined` (never `NaN`), so TypeScript makes you handle it.
+
 ### Enhanced Type Safety
 
 If you want strict type checking for the input value, you can use `parseStrict`.
